Confirmed halt and fail-safe teardown
====================================

Run halt_settlement_test.sh, teardown_test.sh and memory_teardown_test.sh with
bash. CC and CFLAGS select host compiler/sanitizers. Runners extract production
functions; firmware, transport, kernel scheduler and resource allocation are
controlled boundaries. These are not native kernel or QEMU lifecycle tests.

Halt settlement requires confirmation of every assigned CPU and orphaned park
tracking before publishing LOADED. Requested, voluntary and supported force
halt failures keep the existing non-reusable state. A lost ACK is not remote
cancellation. Unsupported architectural force stop returns -EOPNOTSUPP; marker
arming failure must not send force-stop interrupts. CPU ownership tests also
cover an assigned CPU missing from park tracking and the pre-first-spawn host
park slot.

After CPU confirmation, mk_ipi_poll dispatches one captured local-inbox prefix
before permitting reuse. A claimed but unpublished slot returns -EBUSY and
retains resources; it is never skipped or cleared. Existing PCI admission pins
and revocation then protect dispatched requests across grant/ID reuse. See
README.pciback-lifetime for the bounded-drain tests and remaining assumptions.

Repark retirement additionally requires a destination-loop self-repark claim.
The old mailbox clears ready before jumping away, so its claim alone permits
premature reuse of the old park page/page table. repark_arrival_test.sh exercises
the production publication and tracking paths with delayed destination arrival:
source claim plus destination timeout must retain tracking, while successful
arrival needs both claims. This is a controlled mailbox boundary, not native
assembly execution or a reproduced physical race. The park ABI is unchanged.

Image unload admission is centralized before kimage_free() unlinks anything.
ACTIVE is refused. FAILED retains its Image until stop confirmation succeeds.
Uncertain published memory/device operations also pin the instance even after
CPU stop; README.resource-transfers describes this reboot-only quarantine.
The Image locking fixture covers these paths plus failed load and manifest
finalization. Normal/crash callers remain outside the multikernel resource
lock, and their successful free returns zero.

FAILED is not evidence that no CPU executed: an unsuccessful spawn may have
run preparation code or moved CPUs onto instance control memory. An architecture
must not return a failed spawn with an uncancelled child-Image launch that can
arrive after confirmation. The current x86 boot publication revokes an unclaimed
wake on timeout and treats a concurrent claim as success; the RISC-V path returns
the final SBI start result directly, relying on accurate firmware responses.
Neither has a later error point after reported acceptance of the boot launch.
All-owned/tracked-CPU confirmation and separate control-memory retirement remain
required. This contract assumes exclusive ownership and a quiescent previous run;
it does not authorize retrying an ACTIVE instance after a lost shutdown ACK.

Destruction requires READY with no Image. Stop/repark proof precedes IDR or
kernfs removal. Registry locking is dropped for firmware waits and kernfs
draining, while the resource lock excludes competing controllers. Resource
retirement is synchronous; only the final record and persistent boot pages
await lookup reference release and an RCU grace period. A refused memory release
keeps the pool, region metadata and control allocation intact. The memory test
can characterize a saved production revision with MK_CORE_REV=<commit>.

Run kernfs_snapshot_test.sh with sh to check the production root and instance
device_tree read callbacks. Each generation holds the resource mutex; contended
reads return -EAGAIN so a controller holding that mutex can drain kernfs without
waiting on a reader blocked behind it. Retry after contention clears. The mutex
is released before copying the generated buffer. Separate partial reads still
generate separate snapshots, not a stable snapshot for the whole open file.
The fixture checks exclusion at a stubbed DT generator, controller-held-lock
contention, retry, generation errors, offsets and buffer release using a host
pthread mutex. It does not exercise the real DT emitter or native kernfs drain.

The unused bulk kernfs cleanup entry point refuses live boot records, loaded
Images and uncertain states. It does not supply a new inbox/IRQ retirement
contract. Unexpected final-reference release without stop proof retains
allocations rather than reclaiming executing memory; that diagnostic fallback
is not a recoverable management API or proof of leak-free error handling.

Voluntary HALTED work is allocated with the instance record, before publication.
IRQ admission takes a reference to that exact record and coalesces duplicates
through an atomic pending flag; it does not allocate or resolve the ID later.
The pending flag remains set while the worker holds the resource mutex and
attempts stop confirmation. It excludes a new start, including a start after
another controller has settled the same record. Completion clears the flag and
drops the reference. Failed confirmation retains ACTIVE/FAILED and allocations;
a queue rejection drops the reference without publishing a reusable state.
The execute caller must still confirm stop and drain the inbox before copying
an Image, as before. This is not a wire session or peer-authentication protocol.

Remaining qualification gates include native x86 concurrency/lockdep, QEMU
OpenSBI HSM transitions, foreign ring producers, driver/DMA quiescence, stale
queued notifications across re-spawns, and 50-cycle same/different Image reuse.
No #7 session protocol, wire-layout change or mixed-peer compatibility claim
is introduced. RISC-V runtime additionally needs the explicitly separate
modern #26 loader and #27/#28 test integration dependencies.

Pool unmapping is fallible. Control-block unmap failure retains memory-region
metadata, the control allocation and the instance pool, and propagates through
re-reservation and baseline replacement. Destroy attempts retirement before
removing the record from kernfs/IDR; memory readers remain excluded by the
controller mutex. CPU/device return can already have completed on an error.
Unexpected failure after the final reference retains the record/allocations
as a diagnostic leak; it is not a recovery API. The x86 pool-control wrapper
also refuses to free physical memory after an unexpected unmap error.
